lly. The only non-reversible effect is an agent or human having already acted on a compact response it misread as complete during the window before consumer prompts are updated.\\n- Leading indicators and failure signals: `tests/test_tool_output_schemas.py` failing post-change (schema ceiling tripped); `plan-validator.md`-dispatched validation passing a plan/task that should fail structural checks (or vice versa) once its reads return compact stubs; a dispatched worker's CLI `plan read` output silently exceeding the harness cap after #3058 ships (would indicate the ledger branch was missed).\\n- Paired baseline/candidate comparison: for each of the five surfaces plus the ledger-branch equivalents, compare the same oversized plan/task/artifact's response before and after the change \u2014 same input, both code paths (content-store and ledger-held) \u2014 to confirm nothing addressable was dropped (R2) and the compact form's stated retrieval call actually returns the missing content.\\n- Post-change review owner and trigger: implementer of #3058 re-dispatches each of the eight named agent/skill files against oversized fixtures once the change lands, per Verification column above; item owner records the #3058/#3078 and `_paginate_results`-migration scope decisions before task decomposition, per the two Unknown entries above.\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [x] Every material propagation path has evidence or an explicit unknown\\n- [x] Producers, consumers, state owners, and downstream decisions checked\\n- [x] Tests, docs, config, CI, operations, and agent instructions checked\\n- [x] Applicable data, model, prompt, context, people, process, and control effects checked\\n- [x] Transition, rollback, delayed effects, and observability checked\\n- [x] Replacement or removal preserves the purpose of existing capabilities and controls (no replacement/removal in this change; delivery-layer only)\\n</div>\\n\\n## Dependencies\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-20T11:30:20.649293Z</sub>\\n\\n- **In scope for this item, not a separate item**: `dh_core/operations.py:477-515` (`get_ready_tasks`) must actually branch on `full`. This is the mechanism behind `sam_plan(action=\\\"ready\\\")`'s unbounded content-store-branch behavior, and `sam_plan(action=\\\"ready\\\")` is one of this item's three named baseline surfaces (Impact Radius, Change Frame). Deferring it to a separate item would let #3058 ship without meeting its own \\\"compact form automatically\\\" criterion for that surface.\\n- **Blocked on owner scope decisions before task decomposition** (see Blockers / Questions for Human): the `artifact_read` boundary with #3078, the overlap with #2515, and whether the three-way token-budget consolidation belongs inside this item.\\n- **Parent**: #3054.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Priority\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-20T11:30:20.649179Z</sub>\\n\\n**P0** \u2014 already recorded in Context (\u00a77.0: \\\"unbounded responses against a hard harness cap fail rather than degrade\\\"). Root-Cause Analysis corroborates with frequency evidence: this is the third-plus occurrence of the same problem class in this repo's history (#1664, closed 2025 as part of PR #2495; #2515, opened 2025, still in-progress; #3095, opened 2026-08-21) \u2014 a recurring pattern, not a one-off, per the Issue Classification's recurring-pattern determination. A hard failure (not a graceful degrade) against a fixed harness cap, recurring across at least three prior items, keeps this at P0 rather than a lower severity.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Benefits\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-20T11:30:20.649222Z</sub>\\n\\n- Converts a hard failure into a graceful paginated read: unbounded responses against a fixed harness cap fail outright today; bounding them applies R3's compact-by-default behavior on both the content-store and ledger branches and both the MCP and CLI channels.\\n- Closes a recurring pattern rather than adding a fourth ad-hoc fix: Root-Cause Analysis found 3+ prior occurrences of this same problem class (#1664, #2515, #3095); routing through one shared engine removes the recurring need to special-case bounding for each new read surface.\\n- Restores the \\\"compact by default\\\" guarantee `sam_plan(action=\\\"ready\\\")` already claims to have: fixes the dead `full` parameter in `get_ready_tasks` (`dh_core/operations.py:477-515`) rather than leaving that documented behavior silently unimplemented.\\n- Gives every future read surface one existing routing point to adopt by default instead of reimplementing token-budget logic \u2014 the outcome R1 in `agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md` requires.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-20T11:01:16.499316Z</sub>\\n\\n<details><summary>struck: 2026-09-20T12:20:47.056579Z \u2014 Owner ruled the MISSION_DIVERGENT verdict wrong on 2026-09-20; recomputing the verdict and recording the rebuttal in place, per the canonical write-back rule</summary>\\n\\n**MISSION_DIVERGENT**\\n\\n#3058's acceptance criteria route plan, task, and artifact reads through the markdown engine, which aligns with the stated mission in `agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md` R1. However, the issue scopes only to the *new* read paths and does not address the architectural violation that R1 already forbids.\\n\\n**The Mission Statement**\\n\\n`agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md` R1 explicitly mandates: \\\"A single markdown engine serves every markdown consumption path. No component may hand-build a table of contents, a section listing, a content excerpt, or a bounded response of its own. Where such an implementation exists today it is deleted, not maintained in parallel. **A second implementation is a defect regardless of whether it works.**\\\"\\n\\n`component-architecture.md` designates `progressive_markdown` as the single engine and documents current adoption as partial: \\\"Current consumers: backlog's dot-ordinal mapper and token-window extractor. The default item read, the general navigator, plan/task reads, artifact reads, and CLI address navigation are not yet routed through the engine; #3057, #3058, #3059, #3078, and #3063 track those consumer migrations.\\\"\\n\\n**The Current Violation**\\n\\nThe root-cause analysis in this item documents three independent token-budget implementations in the codebase that none of them route through the engine:\\n1. `backlog_core/server.py:231` \u2014 `_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET = 4_000` for `backlog_view`\\n2. `backlog_core/server.py:1659` \u2014 `_resolve_effective_limit` for `backlog_list`\\n3. `sam_schema/server_plan_ops.py:131` \u2014 `_paginate_results` with binary search\\n\\nThese coexist today, which is already a violation of R1. The root-cause analysis improvement statement recommends: \\\"Consolidate all five token-budget pagination surfaces to route through `progressive_markdown`'s shared `ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator`, `ProgressiveDisclosure`, or similar collection engine, with single-source-of-truth constant `_DEFAULT_BUDGET`.\\\"\\n\\n**The Alignment Gap**\\n\\n#3058's acceptance criteria (\\\"Plan read, task read, and artifact content read route through the markdown engine\\\") read as implementation of new engine routing without addressing the migration of the three existing independent implementations. The issue's scope description and acceptance criteria do not explicitly exclude maintaining parallel implementations. The SCOPE_EXPANSION section warns: \\\"implementing this issue without migrating `sam_schema/server_plan_ops.py::_paginate_results` to the engine's documented drop-in replacement **adds a fourth**\\\" independent implementation to the three that already exist.\\n\\nImplementing new read paths that route through the engine, while leaving the three existing implementations in place and operational, creates precisely the \\\"second implementation\\\" R1 names as \\\"a defect regardless of whether it works.\\\" The engine gains new consumers while the problem class it was designed to solve \u2014 token-budget pagination scattered across the codebase \u2014 remains unsolved.\\n\\n**What Alignment Requires**\\n\\nImplementing #3058 in alignment with R1 requires that the work:\\n1. Routes the new plan, task, and artifact reads through `progressive_markdown` (as proposed)\\n2. Consolidates the three existing independent implementations to import from that same engine\\n3. Removes or refactors the hand-built pagination paths in `backlog_core/server.py:231`, `backlog_core/server.py:1659`, and `sam_schema/server_plan_ops.py:131` to become wrappers around the engine's `ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator`, `Paginator`, or `token_bounded` utilities\\n4. Establishes `_DEFAULT_BUDGET` as the single constant across all five surfaces\\n\\nWithout consolidating the existing implementations, the issue ships a \\\"second implementation\\\" (or third, or fourth, counting the ones already there), confirming R1's design intent is divergent from the scope as written.\\n</details>\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-20T12:20:47.056603Z</sub>\\n\\n**MISSION_ALIGNED** \u2014 owner ruling, 2026-09-20. Supersedes the alignment-analyst's MISSION_DIVERGENT verdict.\\n\\n**Owner's words:** \\\"Not needed \u2014 verdict is wrong.\\\" Selected against the stated reason: the three constants serve different limits, so R1's one-engine rule does not require a single value.\\n\\n**What the analyst argued.** R1 states: \\\"A single markdown engine serves every markdown consumption path\u2026 Where such an implementation exists today it is deleted, not maintained in parallel. A second implementation is a defect regardless of whether it works.\\\" The analyst held that #3058 routes only its *new* read paths through the engine while three existing token-budget implementations stay in place, so the item ships a parallel implementation. It required four things for alignment: (1) route the new reads through the engine, (2) consolidate the three existing implementations, (3) refactor the hand-built pagination paths into engine wrappers, (4) establish `_DEFAULT_BUDGET` as the single constant across all five surfaces.\\n\\n**Why requirement 4 is rejected.** The constants are not competing values for one limit. `progressive_markdown/models.py:15` `_DEFAULT_BUDGET` is the *response* budget \u2014 `MAX_MCP_OUTPUT_TOKENS - 500`, fallback 9,500 \u2014 and is R2's single constant for pagination. `backlog_core/server.py:231` `_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET = 4_000` and `:226` `_LIST_TOKEN_BUDGET = 4_400` are R3 *compaction* thresholds: the point at which a response switches to compact form, deliberately far below the response ceiling. Collapsing them to one number would either stop compaction from ever triggering or shrink every paginated page to 4,000 tokens. R1 governs how many engines render markdown; it does not require every caller to pass the same budget argument. A one-engine rule is satisfied by one implementation taking a budget parameter, not by one value.\\n\\n**Why the remaining requirements do not make this item divergent.** Requirements 2 and 3 concern surfaces the impact-analyst deliberately excluded \u2014 the Impact Radius Excluded Candidates section lists `backlog_core/server.py::backlog_view` as out of scope with its reason. Excluded scope recorded with a stated reason is a scope boundary, not a divergence. The one substantive warning worth carrying is narrower and stands: migrating `sam_schema/server_plan_ops.py::_paginate_results` to the engine's documented drop-in replacement (`progressive_markdown/list_navigator.py:539`) is inside this item's blast radius, and implementing around it rather than through it would add a fourth independent implementation. That is covered by requirement 1, which the acceptance criteria already carry.\\n\\n**Open, not blocking:** whether the two `backlog_core` compaction thresholds and `sam_schema/server_plan_ops.py:55` should eventually route their pagination through the engine while keeping their own thresholds. That is a separate question from this item's scope and was not put to the owner.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n<details><summary>struck: 2026-09-20T11:39:41.467397Z \u2014 Superseding the initial draft acceptance criteria with grooming-informed criteria that account for RT-ICA's APPROVED-WITH-GAPS scope questions (artifact_read boundary with #3078, overlap with #2515, token-budget consolidation) without silently adopting or contradicting the Design Intent Alignment MISSION_DIVERGENT scope finding.</summary>\\n\\n- [ ] Plan read, task read, and artifact content read route through the markdown engine\\n- [ ] Each returns the compact form automatically when over budget, per R3\\n- [ ] A listing operation does not embed unbounded bodies\\n- [ ] No response can exceed the configured budget regardless of stored document size\\n</details>\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-20T11:39:41.467420Z</sub>\\n\\n<details><summary>struck: 2026-09-20T13:33:30.463284Z \u2014 Adds the _paginate_results migration, which was the one in-scope constraint recorded nowhere a planner reads, and settles criterion 7 against the owner rulings</summary>\\n\\n- [ ] `sam_plan(action=\\\"read\\\")`, `sam_plan(action=\\\"ready\\\")`, and `sam_task(action=\\\"read\\\")` each return a response within the configured token budget when queried against a record engineered to exceed it, on both the content-store and ledger-backed branches.\\n- [ ] The SAM CLI's `plan read` and `plan ready` commands return the same bounded form as the equivalent MCP call for the same plan/task.\\n- [ ] `get_ready_tasks(..., full=False)` returns the documented compact seven-field form; a test fails if it reverts to a full `Task` dump.\\n- [ ] `sam_plan(action=\\\"ready\\\")` against a plan with several oversized ready tasks returns a response within budget regardless of aggregate task-body size.\\n- [ ] No content addressed by a compact/paginated response is unreachable \u2014 every further-call path a compact response states actually returns the omitted content.\\n- [ ] `test_tool_output_schemas.py`'s existing ceilings still pass after the response schema changes.\\n- [ ] Whether `artifact_read`, the three-way token-budget consolidation, and the #2515 overlap fall inside this item's closing criteria is explicitly recorded (per the owner decisions in Questions for Human) before the item is marked done \u2014 not assumed either way by default.\\n</details>\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-20T13:33:30.463308Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] `sam_plan(action=\\\"read\\\")`, `sam_plan(action=\\\"ready\\\")`, and `sam_task(action=\\\"read\\\")` each return a response within the configured token budget when queried against a record engineered to exceed it, on both the content-store and ledger-backed branches.\\n- [ ] `sam_schema/server_plan_ops.py::_paginate_results` is migrated onto the engine's documented drop-in replacement at `progressive_markdown/list_navigator.py:539`, not reimplemented alongside it. Implementing around it adds a fourth independent pagination implementation, which R1 names a defect regardless of whether it works.\\n- [ ] The SAM CLI's `plan read` and `plan ready` commands return the same bounded form as the equivalent MCP call for the same plan/task.\\n- [ ] `get_ready_tasks(..., full=False)` returns the documented compact seven-field form; a test fails if it reverts to a full `Task` dump. Note `dh_core/operations.py:477-515` accepts `full` today and never branches on it.\\n- [ ] `sam_plan(action=\\\"ready\\\")` against a plan with several oversized ready tasks returns a response within budget regardless of aggregate task-body size.\\n- [ ] No content addressed by a compact or paginated response is unreachable \u2014 every further-call path such a response names actually returns the omitted content.\\n- [ ] `test_tool_output_schemas.py`'s existing ceilings still pass after the response schema changes.\\n- [ ] The #2515 boundary on `sam_plan(action=\\\"read\\\")` is recorded on both items before this item closes.\\n\\n**Settled, no longer open questions.** `artifact_read` belongs to #3078 (`docs/component-architecture.md:147,151`). Consolidating the three token-budget implementations onto one constant is out of scope by owner ruling of 2026-09-20 \u2014 the constants serve different limits, and the reasoning is in Design Intent Alignment. Neither is a closing criterion here.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Expected Behavior\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-20T11:30:20.649241Z</sub>\\n\\n- `sam_plan(action=\\\"read\\\"|\\\"ready\\\")`, `sam_task(action=\\\"read\\\")`, and `artifact_read` each return a response within the configured token budget regardless of the stored document's size or the ready-task count, on both the content-store and ledger-backed branches, and via both the MCP tool and the SAM CLI's `plan read`/`plan ready` commands.\\n- When a response would exceed budget, the surface automatically returns the compact/paginated form (R3) rather than erroring or silently truncating.\\n- No content is dropped: every part of an oversized document remains reachable through a further call (R2).\\n- `get_ready_tasks(..., full=False)` returns the documented compact seven-field form rather than a full `Task` dump.\\n- A same-task read looks the same regardless of which channel (MCP vs. CLI) or which storage branch (content-store vs. ledger) answered it.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Effort\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-20T11:30:20.649309Z</sub>\\n\\nNot sized in calendar time \u2014 scale is bounded by surface count and by the owner resolving the three open scope questions first, not by duration. Known scope: five response-shaping surfaces (`sam_plan` read/ready, `sam_task` read, `artifact_read`, ready-task entry-count bounding) each spanning two storage backings (content-store and ledger) and, for the `plan read`/`ready` commands, two channels (MCP and CLI) \u2014 plus one pre-existing dead-code defect to fix in the same pass (`get_ready_tasks`'s `full` parameter) and eight consumer prompt contracts to re-validate against oversized fixtures post-change (Impact Radius, Transition/Rollback/Observability). Code - Producers evidence shows the five surfaces are independent response-shaping code paths with no shared mutable state, so once scope is fixed the per-surface fixes are parallelizable across agents; the token-budget consolidation and `artifact_read` migration (if in scope) add work proportional to however many of the three open scope questions resolve toward inclusion.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Reproducibility\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-20T11:30:20.649143Z</sub>\\n\\n**Deterministic given a fixed input size** \u2014 this is a recurring-pattern item, not a single crash; reproduction demonstrates the current unbounded-response defect this item aims to close.\\n\\n1. Select or construct a SAM plan/task whose stored content (e.g. a `Completion Report` or `Orchestrator Response` section) exceeds the harness's effective per-call token cap.\\n2. Read it via `sam_plan(config={\\\"action\\\": \\\"read\\\"})` or `sam_task(config={\\\"action\\\": \\\"read\\\"})` (MCP), or `<sam_cli/> plan read --address P/T` (CLI) \u2014 the full stored content returns in one response with no token-budget check (`sam_schema/server.py:95,167`; `sam_schema/sam_plan.py:1399,1605`).\\n3. Call `sam_plan(config={\\\"action\\\": \\\"ready\\\"})` against a plan with several ready tasks whose bodies are individually large \u2014 every task body returns at once. `dh_core/operations.py:477-515` documents a compact seven-field form when `full=False` but never branches on `full`, so this never trims regardless of the caller's intent.\\n4. Import the same plan into the work ledger (e.g. via `/dh:implement-feature`) and repeat steps 2-3 \u2014 the same unbounded behavior reproduces through `dh_core/ledger/transitions.py::read` (:927-963) and `dh_core/ledger/queries.py::ready` (:134-146, `full: bool = True` default), confirming the defect exists on both the content-store and ledger branches.\\n5. Call `artifact_read(item_id=..., artifact_type=\\\"architect\\\")` against a large architecture spec \u2014 the full document returns with no size guard.\\n\\nEach step fails the same way on every run against the same input: a hard response-size trip, not an intermittent one.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Priority**: P0 \u2014 unbounded responses against a hard harness cap fail rather than degrade\\n- Intended behaviour is defined in `plugins/development-harness/docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md`. Component ownership is defined in `plugins/development-harness/docs/component-architecture.md`. Parent: #3054.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Root-Cause Analysis\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-20T10:47:42.345910Z</sub>\\n\\n<details><summary>struck: 2026-09-20T11:26:25.527176Z \u2014 Superseded by corrected entry 2026-09-20T11:22:56.846761Z with accurate dates and citations</summary>\\n\\n**Method**: 6-sigma frequency analysis via backlog history search\\n\\n**Frequency**: 3+ occurrences across related backlog items, tracking token-budget pagination and bounded-response issues\\n\\n**Matches**:\\n- #1664 \u2014 fix: backlog_view dumps entire item body, causing ~14k token MCP responses that fill agent context \u2014 2025 (closed as part of PR #2495)\\n- #2515 \u2014 feat: sam_plan read needs compact/paginated mode \u2014 2025 (in-progress)\\n- #3095 \u2014 docs: backlog_view map=True's \\\"always under 2,000 tokens\\\" claim is unenforced \u2014 2026-08-21 (opened)\\n\\n**Pattern**: Developers independently implement or identify token-budget pagination for individual read surfaces without enforcing routing through the shared `progressive_markdown` engine. Each surface (backlog_view, sam_plan, sam_task, artifact_read, backlog_get_ready_sam_tasks) either applies its own ad-hoc budget constant (`_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET` at `backlog_core/server.py:231`, `_TOKEN_BUDGET` at `sam_schema/server_plan_ops.py:54`) or documents a claimed budget witho